  "explanation": {
    "markdown": "## Auxiliary verbs: do, be and have\n\nIn English, there are three main auxiliary verbs: **do, be** and **have**. They have negative and question forms. Other verbs need an auxiliary to make these forms.  \n  \nModal verbs ([**can**](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a1/can-cant/), [**could**](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/b1/can-could-be-able-to/), [**may, might**](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a2/may-and-might-whats-the-difference/), [**must**](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a2/have-to-dont-have-to-must-mustnt/), [**should**](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a2/should-shouldnt/), [**will**](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a1/will-and-shall/), **would**) are also auxiliary verbs.  \n  \n![Grammar chart of auxiliary verbs do, be and have with forms, tenses and example sentences](/uploads/grammar/a2/auxiliary-verbs-do-be-and-have/img-1.png)\n\n## Be\n\n#### Present and past continuous\n\nWe use **be** + –**ing** verb to form the present and past continuous.  \n  \nWe use **am/is/are** + **\\-ing** to form the [present continuous](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a1/present-continuous/).\n\n-   *She **is reading** a book.*\n-   *They **aren’t watching** TV.*\n-   ***Are** you **studying** for the test?*\n\nWe use **was/were** + **\\-ing** to form the [past continuous](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a2/past-continuous-past-simple/).\n\n-   *I **was cooking** dinner at 8 o’clock.*\n-   *He **wasn’t sleeping**.*\n-   ***Were** they **playing** football?*\n\n#### Present and past simple passive\n\nWe use **be** + past participle to form the [present and past simple passive](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a2/present-past-simple-passive/).\n\nWe use **am/is/are** + past participle to form the present simple passive.\n\n-   *English **is spoken** in many countries.*\n-   *The windows **aren’t cleaned** very often.*\n-   ***Is** the homework **finished**?*\n\nWe use **was/were** + past participle to form the past simple passive.\n\n-   *The match **was cancelled**.*\n-   *The emails **weren’t sent**.*\n-   ***Were** the tickets **booked** online?*\n\n## Do\n\nWe use **do/did** + infinitive to make present and past simple negatives and questions.  \n  \nWe use **don’t/doesn’t** + infinitive to make present simple negatives, and **do/does** + subject + infinitive to make [present simple](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a1/present-simple/) questions.\n\n-   *I **don’t like** spicy food.*\n-   *She **doesn’t work** on Saturdays.*\n-   ***Do** they **live** near here?*\n\nWe use **didn’t** + infinitive to make past simple negatives, and **did** + subject + infinitive to make [past simple](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a2/past-simple-form-use/) questions.\n\n-   *We **didn’t go** to the party.*\n-   *He **didn’t understand** the question.*\n-   ***Did** you **finish** the report?*\n\n## Have\n\nWe use **have** + past participle (**worked, played, seen, etc.**) to form the present and past perfect.  \n  \nWe use **have/has** + past participle to form the [present perfect](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a2/present-perfect/).\n\n-   *She **has finished** her homework.*\n-   *They **haven’t visited** the museum.*\n-   ***Have** you **seen** this film?*\n\nWe use **had** + past participle to form the [past perfect](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a2/past-perfect/).\n\n-   *I **had left** before the meeting started.*\n-   *She **hadn’t studied** for the exam.*\n-   ***Had** they **arrived** when you called?*",
